## How Auto-Configuration is Loaded

At startup, `AutoConfigurationImportSelector` reads the auto-configuration candidates from:
- **Spring Boot 3:** `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports`
- **Spring Boot 2:** `META-INF/spring.factories` under key `EnableAutoConfiguration`

Each entry is a fully-qualified class name. Spring Boot loads all of them but only applies those whose conditions pass.

## Conditions That Control Activation

| Annotation | Activates when... |
|---|---|
| `@ConditionalOnClass` | a specific class is on the classpath |
| `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` | no bean of that type exists yet |
| `@ConditionalOnProperty` | a property matches a given value |
| `@ConditionalOnWebApplication` | running in a web context |

A typical auto-configuration class looks like:
```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {
    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties props) {
        return props.initializeDataSourceBuilder().build();
    }
}
```

## Overriding Auto-Configuration

Define your own bean and auto-configuration steps aside:
```java
@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() {
    HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
    ds.setMaximumPoolSize(50);
    return ds;
}
```

To disable an entire auto-configuration:
```java
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)
```

## Production Reality

The conditions evaluation report (`debug=true`) is your best debugging tool. When a bean isn't being created, the report tells you which condition failed — saves hours of guessing.

## What @EnableAutoConfiguration Does

```java
@Import(AutoConfigurationImportSelector.class)
public @interface EnableAutoConfiguration {
    Class<?>[] exclude() default {};
    String[] excludeName() default {};
}
```

The `@Import(AutoConfigurationImportSelector.class)` line is the real work. This selector reads all auto-configuration candidates and filters them based on conditions at startup.

## Inside @SpringBootApplication

`@SpringBootApplication` is a meta-annotation:
```java
@SpringBootConfiguration   // = @Configuration
@EnableAutoConfiguration   // triggers auto-config
@ComponentScan             // scans packages
public @interface SpringBootApplication { ... }
```

Most Spring Boot apps never use `@EnableAutoConfiguration` directly.

## Excluding Auto-Configurations

Two ways to exclude:
```java
// By class (preferred — compile-time safe)
@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)

// By name (use when class isn't on classpath)
@SpringBootApplication(excludeName = "org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration")
```

Or in `application.properties`:
```properties
spring.autoconfigure.exclude=org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceAutoConfiguration
```

## Production Reality

If you're writing a non-web application (batch job, CLI tool), you'll often exclude `WebMvcAutoConfiguration` and `EmbeddedWebServerFactoryCustomizerAutoConfiguration` to prevent Spring from starting an HTTP server.

## The Three Annotations

```java
@SpringBootConfiguration  // marks as config source (@Configuration equivalent)
@EnableAutoConfiguration  // loads auto-config classes from imports file
@ComponentScan            // scans current package + sub-packages
public @interface SpringBootApplication { ... }
```

## Component Scan Scope

If your main class is in `com.example.myapp`, Spring scans:
- `com.example.myapp` and all sub-packages ✅
- `com.example.other` — NOT scanned ❌

This is the most common startup mistake — components in a sibling package not being found.

## Useful Attributes

```java
@SpringBootApplication(
    exclude = { DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class },
    scanBasePackages = { "com.example.myapp", "com.example.shared" }
)
public class Application { ... }
```

## Production Reality

Keep the main class at the root of your package tree. It's a convention the whole team should know — a new developer who moves the main class to a sub-package will break component scanning silently. Components will compile but won't be found at runtime.

## Basic Usage

```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {
    // Only loaded if DataSource is on classpath
}
```

## value vs name

```java
// value: compile-time class reference (fails if class not on compile classpath)
@ConditionalOnClass(value = MongoClient.class)

// name: string — safe when class may not be on compile classpath
@ConditionalOnClass(name = "com.mongodb.client.MongoClient")
```

For library/starter authors, `name` is safer because the class they're checking for belongs to an optional dependency.

## The Starter Pattern

```xml
<!-- Adding this dependency... -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-redis</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

This brings `RedisConnectionFactory` onto the classpath, which triggers:
```java
@ConditionalOnClass(RedisConnectionFactory.class)
public class RedisAutoConfiguration { ... }
```

## Production Reality

`@ConditionalOnClass` is evaluated before the bean graph is built, during the condition evaluation phase of context refresh. It's cheap — just a classpath check. Don't confuse it with `@ConditionalOnBean`, which checks the already-built application context.

## Basic Usage

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnBean(CacheManager.class)
public CacheMetricsPublisher cacheMetrics(CacheManager cacheManager) {
    return new CacheMetricsPublisher(cacheManager);
}
// Only created if a CacheManager bean exists
```

## Ordering is Critical

`@ConditionalOnBean` evaluates against beans already registered at the time of condition check. In auto-configuration, use `@AutoConfigureAfter` to declare ordering:

```java
@AutoConfiguration
@AutoConfigureAfter(CacheAutoConfiguration.class)
@ConditionalOnBean(CacheManager.class)
public class CacheMetricsAutoConfiguration {
    // CacheAutoConfiguration runs first, then this checks for CacheManager
}
```

## Checking by Type vs Name

```java
// By type (most common)
@ConditionalOnBean(DataSource.class)

// By bean name
@ConditionalOnBean(name = "primaryDataSource")

// By annotation on the bean
@ConditionalOnBean(annotation = Primary.class)
```

## Production Reality

Avoid `@ConditionalOnBean` in regular `@Configuration` classes in your application code — it's hard to reason about bean ordering there. It's designed for auto-configuration classes where ordering is explicit. For application-level optionality, use constructor injection with `Optional<T>` or `@Autowired(required = false)` instead.

## How It Works

```java
// Spring Boot's auto-configuration
@AutoConfiguration
public class DataSourceAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public DataSource dataSource(DataSourceProperties props) {
        return props.initializeDataSourceBuilder().build();
    }
}
```

You override it by simply defining your own:
```java
@Configuration
public class DatabaseConfig {
    @Bean  // no special annotation needed
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
        ds.setMaximumPoolSize(50);
        return ds;
    }
}
```

Spring Boot sees your `DataSource`, and `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` causes the auto-configured one to be skipped.

## Matching Strategies

```java
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)          // by type
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(name = "myDataSource")      // by name
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(annotation = Primary.class) // by annotation
```

## The Same-Class Gotcha

```java
// This does NOT work as expected
@Configuration
public class Config {
    @Bean
    public DataSource myDataSource() { ... }  // defined here

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean  // evaluated before myDataSource is registered
    public DataSource defaultDataSource() { ... }  // ALSO creates a bean!
}
// Result: duplicate DataSource — likely a NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException
```

## Production Reality

When you're debugging why your custom bean isn't being used, first check whether the auto-configured bean also has `@ConditionalOnMissingBean`. If it does and your bean isn't overriding it, check that your `@Configuration` class is component-scanned before the auto-configuration runs.

## Basic Usage

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnProperty(
    name = "app.cache.enabled",
    havingValue = "true",
    matchIfMissing = false
)
public CacheManager cacheManager() {
    return new RedisCacheManager(...);
}
```

```properties
# application.properties
app.cache.enabled=true
```

## matchIfMissing Behaviour

| Property set? | havingValue | matchIfMissing | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| No | "true" | false | Condition FAILS (bean not created) |
| No | "true" | true | Condition PASSES (bean created) |
| "true" | "true" | either | Condition PASSES |
| "false" | "true" | either | Condition FAILS |

Use `matchIfMissing = true` for features that should be on by default.

## Feature Flag Pattern

```java
@Configuration
@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "feature.audit.enabled", havingValue = "true")
public class AuditConfiguration {
    @Bean public AuditEventRepository auditRepository() { ... }
    @Bean public AuditService auditService() { ... }
    // All beans in this class toggle together
}
```

## Production Reality

Feature flags via `@ConditionalOnProperty` are a clean way to handle gradual rollouts and A/B deployments. However, they're evaluated at startup — changing a property requires a restart. For runtime feature toggling without restarts, you need a different approach (like Spring Cloud Config + `@RefreshScope`).
